3.1 SARIMAX
The SARIMAX (seasonal autoregressive integrated moving average with exogenous regressors) model is a potent generalization of the ARIMA model. We assume \(u_t\), \(t\in\mathbb{Z}\), to be iid random variables with mean zero and finite variance \(\sigma_u^2\). Then the SARIMAX process \(\{Y_t\}_{t\in\mathbb{Z}}\) is defined through \[\begin{equation}
\Phi(B^s)\phi(B)(1-B)^d (1-B^s)^D(Y_t-\mathbf{x}_t^{'}\mathbf{\beta})=\Theta(B^s)\theta(B)u_t,
\end{equation}\] where \(B\) is the so-called backshift operator, so that for any time series variable \(Z_t\) we have \(BZ_t = Z_{t-1}\) and \(B^sZ_t=Z_{t-s}\). \(s\) is a positive integer reflecting the seasonal period. Moreover, \(\Phi(B^s)=1-\sum_{i=1}^{P}\Phi_iB^{is}\), \(\Theta(B^s)=1+\sum_{j=1}^{Q}\Theta_jB^{js}\), \(\phi(B)=1-\sum_{l=1}^{p}\phi_lB^{l}\), and \(\theta(B)=1+\sum_{k=1}^{q}\theta_kB^{k}\) are polynomials without common roots. \(\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_P\), \(\Theta_1,\dots, \Theta_Q\), \(\phi_1,\dots,\phi_p\), and \(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_q\) are all real-valued coefficients. \(d,D \in \mathbb{N}_0\) are the orders of differencing. \(\mathbf{x}_t = (X_{1,t} \hspace{2mm} X_{2,t} \hspace{2mm} \dots \hspace{2mm} X_{m,t})^{'}\) is a vector with values of \(m\) exogenous regressors at time \(t\), and \(\beta=(\beta_1 \hspace{2mm} \dots \hspace{2mm} \beta_m)^{'}\) is a corresponding vector of coefficients. A short-hand for such a process is \(\text{SARIMAX}(p, d, q)(P,D,Q)\), where the terms in the brackets are the model orders.
This model nests the standard \(\text{ARIMAX}(p, d, q)\) for \(P=Q=D=0\): \[\begin{equation}
\phi(B)(1-B)^d (Y_t - \mathbf{x}_t^{'}\mathbf{\beta}) = \theta(B)u_t.
\end{equation}\] Note that one can include a linear time trend by setting the \(r\)th regressor, \(r\in\{1,\dots,m\}\), to \(X_{r,t}=t\), which corresponds to a constant term in the differenced series.
A basic \(\text{ARMA}(p, q)\) model (with non-zero mean \(\mu \in \mathbb{R}\)) is then simply given by \[\begin{equation}
\phi(B) (Y_t - \mu) = \theta(B)u_t.
\end{equation}\] This model is without differencing.
